   "abstract": "<jats:p>The aim of this study was to examine whether repeated testing with feedback benefits learning compared to rereading of introductory psychology key\u2010concepts in an educational context. The testing effect was examined immediately after practice, after 18\u00a0days, and at a five\u2010week delay in a sample of undergraduate students (<jats:italic>n\u00a0</jats:italic>=<jats:italic>\u00a0</jats:italic>83). The results revealed that repeated testing with feedback significantly enhanced learning compared to rereading at all delays, demonstrating that repeated retrieval enhances retention compared to repeated encoding in the short\u2010 and the long\u2010term. In addition, the effect of repeated testing was beneficial for students irrespectively of working\u00a0memory capacity. It is argued that teaching methods involving repeated retrieval are important to consider by the educational system.</jats:p>",
